{
  "chapter": "durability/long-lived-agents",
  "tier": "build",
  "notes": [
    "An entity workflow is one execution per case, alive for the whole lifetime, with every",
    "transition arriving as an event. Between events nothing runs, so three weeks of wall",
    "clock costs a durable timer.",
    "Inbound events arrive in bursts because that is how people type. Handling each one",
    "Pending signals are lost on continue-as-new unless they are drained first, so a recycle",
    "that lands mid-burst has to flush the buffer before it recycles.",
    "separately produces contradictory replies, so accumulate into a buffer, wait for a quiet",
    "window that each new event restarts, then act once on the whole batch.",
    "History is capped. Recycle with headroom, because draining generates events and recycling",
    "at the ceiling can terminate the workflow.",
    "rejected. Entity workflows need quiet moments.",
    "What crosses the boundary is the input, so a recycle forces the compaction decision: a",
    "reference and a cursor over a summary, and a summary over the raw transcript.",
    "Timers must be absolute. A relative sleep restarts on every transition, so the deadline",
    "never arrives.",
    "An entity workflow needs a defined end, or it runs until somebody notices it months later."
  ],
